hi I'm Evan Carmichael and welcome to  another edition of modeling the masters  today we're going to look at how the son  of immigrant laborers went against his  parents dreams of him becoming a  preacher and built one of the most  successful companies in the food  production business this is the story of  Henry Heinz in the top three lessons  that you can learn from his success  Henry John Heinz was an American  businessman founded the HJ Heinz Company  he was the first of eight children born  the German immigrant parents who wanted  him to become a preacher Heinz Walker  had other plans his passion was helping  his mother bottle and sell the pickles  she grew in their yard and he saw his  future in food production by the time he  was just nine years old Heinz was  growing grindy and bottling and selling  his own brand with horseradish sauce at  10:00 punch was given 3/4 of an acre of  land to tent his own garden a 12 he  graduated three and a half acres of land  and is using a horse and cart to make  his deliveries to grocery stores when  the time can reach 17 years old Heinz  was making twenty four hundred dollars a  year from his brother's close to sixty  thousand and today's dollars  Heinz eventually expanded his business  by focusing on making new product lines  include pickles and ketchup  I'm still on as president of the company  he founded until his death at age 74 at  that time the HJ Heinz Company had more  than 25 food processing plants 40,000  acres of land under cultivation and over  6,500 employees by following his passion  and by working hard Heinz was eventually  able to make his parents proud and  dominate his industry to help you  dominate your industry  here are three action items that you can  learn from Henry hi  action I am number one create a catchy  slogan whatever industry you're in you  probably have many competitors how can  you stand out from everyone else a great  way to cut through the clutter is to  create a catchy slogan that highlights  what you're really good at Hines was  looking for a slogan while riding on a  streetcar in New York one day in 1896 he  then saw an advertisement for a shoe  store that red 21 styles putting the  hinds I said to myself  we don't have styles of products but we  do have varieties of models handed up  how many we had accounted well beyond 57  but 57 kept coming back to my mind 7-7  there are so many illustrations of the  psychological influence of that figure  and its alluring significance to people  of all ages and races at 58 varieties or  59 varieties didn't appeal to me as  being equally strong with that Hines  immediately jumped off the streetcar  went down to the print shop and drafted  up a card with the new 57 variety slogan  reflecting back Hines acknowledged I  myself did not realize how highly  successful slogan it was going to be  action item number two be unique with  your promotions another great way to cut  through the clutter and have potential  customers pay attention to you is to be  unique with your promotional campaigns  no tactic was too flashy oh gallery for  Heinz he wanted his products to stand  out in shine literally in 1900 Heinz  decided to erect the first ever electric  sign in New York City on the corner of  Fifth Avenue and 23rd Street in  Manhattan standing six stories tall the  sign was a large electrical it pickle  the for the Heinz name and his 57  varieties slogan in the display room  below the sign Heinz employees could be  seen packing miniature pickles into  bottles the science of 1200 lightbulbs  to build and cost the company some $90  every night but for Heinz it was all  worth it four years is Electric pickle  and fresh shoppers along the famous New  York Strip and served as invaluable  advertising  Hinds was a master promoter and was even  responsible for Pok pioneering one of  the major trends in the industry  obsesses quality freshness and  cleanliness Hinds invented the concept  of the factory tour anyone who is  interested in seeing how high is  produced and packaged his products was  now allowed to witness the product  firsthand he was confident in his  operations and believed opening up for  the public would help build confidence  and trust in his company the move  sparked an outburst of positive  publicity not to mention a wave of  copycats action item number three have a  quality product and be proud of it most  of the famous entrepreneurs achieve  success not because they were trying to  make a lot of money but because they  created a product or service that was  good quality and that they were proud to  make and promote Heinz was very proud of  all of his products in addition to  allowing the public access to his  factories he also opened up his products  with them literally  he was one of the first to ever packages  products in clear glass bottles Heinz's  competitors would often use filler  ingredients to pump up the volume of the  products and then conceal the fact by  using opaque color containers Heinz  wanted his customers to see exactly what  they were buying he was proud of his  products quality and other transparent  bottles to ensure customers they were  getting their money's worth  - biographer Robert C Albert's claimed  that Heinz had hit one of the most  important and shaping business ideas of  his time that a pure article of superior  quality could find a ready market  through its intrinsic value so long as  it was packaged and promoted properly  so remember create a catchy slogan be  unique with your promotions and have a  quality product and be proud of it to  finish up this video I wanted to share  one of my favorite true stories about  Henry Heinz and some of his best quotes  in the banking panic of 1875 a young  Heinz found himself overextended and  bankrupt he had tried unsuccessfully to  turn for help to the grocers he had been  supplying with produce over the past few  years they however turned their backs on  him too he couldn't even get enough  credit to feed his family with that  Heinz decided that he would never again  be placed in a situation where he had to  depend on others to survive he made a  decision to be successful and never  looked back  thank you for joining me for another  edition of modeling the masters if you  enjoyed please give the video a thumbs  up below I'd also love to hear your  feedback if you want to leave a comment  and stay tuned for the next episode  you
 

